<language>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegiments" value="Separatist Regiments"/>
	<!-- vanilla fractions -->
	<entry name="AdeptusMechanicusDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="The last apostate has fallen; the last xenite has been dragged from its hiding place. Their heretical technologies are destined for the smelter; their defiled machines, for the scrapyard. Those who survived will be sent to the Cradle of the Protoservitor to have their minds erased. Xenotechnology did not save them. The Omnissiah does not let heresy go unpunished. Gladius has been cleansed."/>
	<entry name="AdeptusMechanicusDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="Apostates who have defiled the sacred machines through contact with xenotechnologies. The Xenarites found refuge among them—and now they are paying the price. Their re-soldered engines and captured drones are not progress, but an abomination in the eyes of Omnissiah. We will wipe their heretical schematics from the face of the planet. We will study their corpses. And we will burn everything they managed to corrupt."/>
	<entry name="AstraMilitarumDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="The separatist threat has been eliminated. Their heretical devices have been burned, and their leaders beheaded. But the commissars have already noticed that some soldiers have become pensive after fighting them... This heresy must be burned from their minds. Now we must focus our efforts on securing Gladius Prime for the Imperium."/>
	<entry name="AstraMilitarumDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="You’ve encountered a group of separatists—former Imperial Guards who shot down the Aquilas. They’re trying to contact you, preaching about freedom and the “New Humanity.” The Commissar has ordered all frequencies to be jammed. No matter whether they are traitors, heretics or Xenos pawns, they must be wiped from the face of Gladius."/>
	<entry name="ChaosSpaceMarinesDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="Their “New Humanity” has been reduced to ashes. Their drones are dust. Their ideals are fodder for demons. They thought they could stay neutral—neither with the Imperium nor with Chaos. Fools. Their “freedom” did not protect them from us, and their “faith in humanity” is merely a faint echo of what they sought to renounce. In the coming darkness, there is no neutrality. Those who are not with us will become the stepping stones to our ascension. It’s a shame—some of them could have made excellent cultists. Now their souls belong to the Warp, and there they will learn what true freedom is."/>
	<entry name="ChaosSpaceMarinesDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="Ah, the separatists—people who think they could escape the Imperium without paying the price. They fled one tyranny only to build a new one. “New Humanity”—how laughable. Freedom is an illusion that the weak sell themselves to make it easier to die for their new masters. Their faith in the Emperor was their shield—albeit a weak, false one, but a shield nonetheless. Now that shield is gone. Their “New Humanity” is an open door to true freedom. They lacked the courage to accept the gods, but the Emperor’s protection will no longer shield them. We will show them true will—and perhaps some of them will see the light and join us. The rest we will simply kill. Both are a source of amusement."/>
	<entry name="DrukhariDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="Their “New Humanity” has fallen, just like everything else. Their drones are toys, their tanks are scrap metal, their ideals are food for the ur-ghouls. But all is not lost. The few who survived will head to Commorra. Let them see what true freedom is—freedom from hope, from faith, from illusions. Their suffering will be our entertainment. And their bodies—the adornment of our halls. A fine hunt."/>
	<entry name="DrukhariDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="Oh, another group of people has fled the Imperium… boring. They think freedom means choosing a new master, and they believe in their pathetic ideals. It usually ends the same way—death or slavery. But you know what? There’s something amusing about them. They don’t have that fanatical faith that would get in the way of us playing with them. Just pride and illusions. And illusions are so easy to shatter. This is going to be fun. Slow. Delicious. I promise..."/>
	<entry name="EldarDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="Their “New Humanity” fell, just as all empires before them had fallen. They thought they could outwit fate. They were wrong. Their drones are primitive, their tactics are borrowed, and their ideals are more naive than those of children. We did not want this war, but the ways of fate are inexorable. Perhaps next time humanity will understand that you cannot build a future by denying the past. Or perhaps not. That is no longer our concern."/>
	<entry name="EldarDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="Yet another group of people who have rejected their Dead God. They call themselves the “New Humanity”—as if one could start anew without paying for the past. They take pride in their freedom, but freedom without discipline leads to ruin—we know this better than anyone. They fight the Tau drones, adopt their tactics, but reject their faith. Does this make them stronger? Or more vulnerable? Time will tell. For now—we will watch. And, if necessary, we will eliminate the threat they do not yet realize."/>
	<entry name="NecronsDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="Just as predicted, these flesh-and-blood creatures were no match for the immortal metal. We are rounding up the surviving fighters and civilians in their ruined cities and homes. They will become obedient slaves. Analysis has shown that those they called separatists are merely another internal conflict among humans. Their “New Humanity” is no different from the old. Just a typical rebellion. It doesn’t matter. We proceed as usual."/>
	<entry name="NecronsDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="The weak flesh-based race—humans—reproduce rapidly and are deeply rooted in their territory. Their strength lies in their numbers, their war machines, and their battle formations—destroy those, and they will crumble like sand sculptures. Yet these are different from the usual Astra Militarum. Who are they, then? It doesn’t matter; the flesh-covered ones are doing our work for us. Let’s destroy both of them."/>
	<entry name="OrksDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="Wot? We zogged all uv 'em? Already? But we only just got started. Dese oomies really break easy!"/>
	<entry name="OrksDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="Oomies! Squishy, pink oomies. Dey don't put up much of a fight on der own, but wiv a few tanks behind 'em you could 'ave some fun, 'til a better fight comes along. Pity dey squish so easy!"/>
	<entry name="SistersOfBattleDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="Their “New Humanity” has turned to ashes. Their heretical devices have melted into molten metal. Their ideals have been erased. They thought that freedom from the Emperor and an alliance with xenotechnologies would make them stronger. They were wrong. Without faith, a person is nothing. We, though few in number, revealed the truth to them. Too late. Now their souls will stand before the Golden Throne, if He deems them worthy. And we will continue to purify Gladius in the name of the Emperor."/>
	<entry name="SistersOfBattleDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="Heretics. Not those who fell to the whispers of Chaos, but those who consciously rejected the Emperor’s light, despite having been born into it. They have brought down the aquilas, defiled the sacred machines by touching the vile technologies of the xenos, and sworn allegiance to the “New Humanity”—an empty phrase, devoid of divine will. They will not accept our mercy. They deserve only the purifying flame. May the Emperor accept their souls—if they have any left."/>
	<entry name="SpaceMarinesDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="To be honest, even the familiar Astra Militarum were tougher to fight. They thought that freedom from the Emperor and stolen alien technology would make them stronger. They were wrong. Another heresy has been eradicated. May the Emperor rest their souls—if they even deserve it."/>
	<entry name="SpaceMarinesDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="Renegades, former guardsmen who cast aside their aquilas and swore allegiance—to what? An empty ideal, devoid of faith, discipline, and purpose? They adopted the tactics of the xenos, defiled their equipment with heretical modifications, yet never gained either the power of Chaos or the protection of the Emperor. They are nobodies. Nothing but an annoying obstacle on the path to the purification of Gladius. Destroy them without wasting words."/>
	<entry name="TauDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="These Guelas turned out to be more complex than their counterparts from Astra Militarum. They learned so much from us and even managed to build something of their own. But without the Greater Good, any of their achievements are nothing but ashes. We offered them light and a place in the Empire, but they refused. Perhaps the survivors will see the truth. Or their children. The Greater Good is patient. It will wait."/>
	<entry name="TauDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="Oh, the Guel. Pathetic in every way, using Dark Age technology in war, praying… Wait a minute, they’re different. Their equipment has no Aquils, and traces of our technology are visible… ah, no, just drones. But they learned from us; they rejected their Dead God. And yet they have rejected the Greater Good as well? How? Why? They are living proof that our path is the right one, but they are too proud or blind to accept it. We must try to convince them, but they have not responded to our messages."/>
	<entry name="TyranidsDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="Of course, they all died. Of course. They relied on order, ranked troops, heavy armour, artillery, respected generals. Gaunts destroyed their order, genestealers danced through their ranks, bioacid dissolved their armour, gargoyles descended on the artillery, and the Lictors tore their generals apart."/>
	<entry name="TyranidsDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="The Hive Mind flexes its psychic muscles hungrily. If this infinite alien mind were capable of favoritism, these human animals would be its preferred prey. In the birthing chambers lightly armoured Hormagaunts and Lictors are being born to chase down the human infantry, whilst Carnifexes and Exocrines are gestating, so that they might crack open the armoured vehicles, to access the tender interiors."/>
	<!-- Defeated -->
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedAdeptusMechanicus" value="As the last Magos of the Adeptus Mechanicus falls, their troops lose focus and are easily cut down. Looking at the Skitarii corpses, you cannot but feel sad—these were people just like us, yet driven by their faith to their deaths at the hands of their own kind, while other enemies still threaten the New Humanity. Such a waste."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedAstraMilitarum" value="The Imperial Guard’s troops have been routed and decapitated. This is no cause for celebration. Looking at their ranks, we see those who could have become part of the New Humanity. They had the same faces, the same training, the same doubts. But they chose loyalty to the dead Emperor over the right to their own lives; now they, too, are dead, and we cannot help but mourn them. The war with the Imperium continues, and every battle like this is a reminder of the price of freedom."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedChaosSpaceMarines" value="The chaosites are… dead? From lasgun fire and true grit! The separatists are celebrating their victory, but the celebration won’t last long. The soldiers of New Humanity know full well the price of victory over Chaos. Commissars and xenarites have already begun conducting preventive interviews with the personnel, identifying signs of possible infection. Vigilance and purity of mind—that is what will protect us from the taint. It will be a long and difficult purification."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedDrukhari" value="With the last Kabalite death, with the last piece of last Haemonculus incinerated in a plasma burst, an aura of peace descends on the planet. On the ruins of the hives, the sparbats sing again. It is as if Gladius Prime is thanking you… For now, we can still breathe a sigh of relief"/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedEldar" value="However many million years of civilisation and learning and here they lie, all that learning lost, dead in the dust of an alien world. A race in decline, clinging to the past, has fallen. The separatists will not repeat their mistakes—we have already learned from our battles with them, and the Xenarites are studying samples of their technology. Even in the enemy’s defeat, we seek an opportunity to grow stronger. Their legacy, though foreign, will serve the New Humanity."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedNecrons" value="The green light fades from the last artificial eye, as the last Necron gives up the fight. After immeasurable aeons of war, their cities are crushed, their troops atomised, and their leaders slain. Though beneath the planet's surface, millions more still slumber in deathly unquiet. The Xenarites advise us not to wake them. We’ll take their advice, but we’ll install seismic sensors just in case. This isn’t a complete victory, just a delay..."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedOrks" value="The Greenskin menace has been defeated. Though that is not to say that it has been eliminated. Even though every Ork, Grot and Snotling is dead, their corpses are already spreading the spores that will raise a new army of Greenskins in a generation. Rumors are circulating among the Xenarites that somewhere on Tau, there is technology capable of permanently ridding the planet of this plague. We’ll keep that in mind. If we survive this war and our state stands firm, we’ll definitely try to get our hands on that knowledge. For now, armed with laser rifles, flamethrowers, and combat cannons, we’ll wait for them..."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedSistersOfBattle" value="They fought with fanatical faith and zeal, led by the living Saint, against people who simply wanted to live and dreamed of freedom. They had bolters, power armor, and the blessing of their God, while you had only lasguns, body armor, and your own despised comrades. And yet… when the dust settled, it was our regiments that remained standing."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedSpaceMarines" value="The fabled Adeptus Astartes, the legendary warriors of the Imperium, lie dead to a man. No apothecary remains to gather their geneseed for the next generation, no mission from distant Terra can rescue them. We won’t deny it—there’s much to learn from these fallen giants. Their tactics and iron will—we’ll study them just as we studied the Tau. But even their gene seed and power armor couldn’t save them from our coordinated fire. The defense of Gladius Prime is now in our hands, and we will continue on our path, absorbing the best from all our enemies."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedSeparatistRegiments" value="Their commanders remain silent, and their equipment smolders in the fields of Gladius. Deprived of coordination, they quickly fell apart, but refused to surrender—right to the very end. We feel no pride—only bitterness. They could have been part of our common cause. Now we must focus our efforts on defending what started all of this in the first place..."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedTau" value="They were our greatest teachers and our mortal enemies. We fought them while we were still loyal to the Imperium, and we adopted everything that could make us stronger. We studied their drones, their tactics, their coordination. This time, they fell by our hands. They offered us the chance to join their Greater Good, but we have heard of the fate of other races in their Empire. We will not become yet another forgotten flock. It is our duty to preserve humanity, not to hand it over to be slowly torn apart. Thank you for the lesson, teachers; we paid for it with our blood and yours."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDefeatedTyranids" value="With the death of the last synapse creature, the Tyranids have turned from a horrifying army of synchronised killing, into feral animals. Like the Catachan Devils, these animals are still vicious and deadly, but without co-ordination, they do not currently pose a threat to mankind. We cannot leave this threat unaddressed—our leaders are already drawing up plans to exterminate the remaining members of the swarm, while the Xenarites are eager to study this perfect killing machine in order to identify its weaknesses once and for all."/>
	<!-- Discovered -->
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredAdeptusMechanicus" value="The Mechanicus Cult. Once, we fought side by side with them. Now their machine eyes see us only as heretics. The Xenarites warned that the Orthodox would not forgive us for studying xenotechnology. It seems they have sent the Castellans to wipe us from the face of Gladius. It’s a shame. Their knowledge could have been useful to the New Humanity..."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredAstraMilitarum" value="The Imperial Guard, our former brothers. The same uniforms, the same boots, the same faces—they could have been our comrades just yesterday... But they look at us with a hatred that not even a Chaos cultist could inspire. To them, we are a living reminder that the Imperium can be betrayed not only in the name of the dark gods, but simply for the sake of freedom. They won’t listen. They’ll just shoot."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredChaosSpaceMarines" value="Damned! Once they were human—just like us—but they chose the lies of Chaos, while we chose freedom. A chasm divides us, but something unites us—the Imperium hates us both. Only they smile at their hatred, while we grit our teeth. Keep your distance. No deals. Only fire."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredDrukhari" value="We’ve heard stories about the Dark Eldar. Of cities slaughtered down to the last infant. Of tortures that last for centuries. The Xenarites have warned that sadism is not a craft, but their religion. Our astropaths tremble just looking at them. The best we can do is destroy them before they begin their hunt."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredEldar" value="The Eldar... A race in decline, clinging to the past. In some ways, they remind us of the Imperium—the same pride, the same obsession with ancient rituals. But at least they don’t try to convert us to their faith. They simply consider us beneath their notice. Well, we’ll prove them wrong."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredNecrons" value="The Ancients, machines made of metal and hatred. The Xenarites are thrilled—they say their technology might hold the key to immortality. But we don’t need immortality. We need them to fall asleep again—this time, forever. As long as they haven’t awakened their armies, we have a chance. Target their regenerators. Don’t let them rise."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredOrks" value="Orcs are a green avalanche that charges forward without even knowing why. They’re simple—they want a fight, and we’ll give them one. But not in the open field. The Xenarites have studied their psychology: if you survive the first attack, the orcs lose interest and start fighting each other. Let’s use that to our advantage. Coordination, markers, retreat. Let them kill each other."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredSistersOfBattle" value="Followers of Sororitas. Fanatics who see heresy in anyone who doesn’t worship the Emperor. To them, we’re traitors worse than Chaos cultists. Because we left of our own free will, not because we were deceived by demons. Someone suggested using them as slaves in the mines—it’s useless. These women will die, but they won’t break. They don’t surrender, they don’t bargain, they don’t switch sides. They can only be destroyed. Every last one of them. You’ve heard that somewhere on this planet there was their preceptory—destroy it, and their faith will crumble."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredSpaceMarines" value="The Living Gods of War, as they are called in the Imperium. To us, they are just another set of enemies who underestimate the separatists. They expect us to resort to mindless Guards tactics. We will show them that the Guard can be both flexible and deadly. And then their belief in their own superiority will crumble."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredSeparatistRegiments" value="We stumbled upon another group of separatists. They’re supposed to be our allies—but why aren’t they saying anything? Why aren’t they responding to our messages? Maybe this warp storm is driving everyone crazy? Or maybe we have ideological differences with them? We tried to negotiate; we offered to join forces, but they responded with fire. There’s only one thing left to do... It’s a shame... So many casualties—and all just to prove who’s right..."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredTau" value="Our greatest teachers—and our enemies. We fought against them while still loyal to the Imperium, and adopted everything that worked: coordination, markers, tactical flexibility. Now we want to show them that the students have surpassed the masters. Not out of malice—out of respect. But if they stand in the way of the New Humanity, we will wipe them out."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsDiscoveredTyranids" value="A hungry swarm devouring worlds. But here, on Gladius, they lack the support of hive ships. The Xenari confirm it: without the Hive Mind, they lack coordination; their beasts act individually. This is not the unstoppable force feared in other sectors. Perhaps the Guards alone will be enough to stop them."/>
	<!-- Properties and Flavor -->
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsFlavor" value="<style name='Italic'/>“We didn’t flee from the Imperium; we simply stopped dying for a lie. And if the truth demands our lives—well, then so be it. Not the Emperor’s. Not the commissar’s. Ours.”<style name='Default'/><br/><br/>The Separatist regiments are former soldiers of the Astra Militarum who rejected the tyranny of the Imperium and its cult of death. They did not fall to Chaos; they did not sell themselves to the xenos; they simply grew tired of being cannon fodder for senseless orders and a faith that never protected them. The Separatists’ technology differs little from that of the Imperium—the same lasguns, the same tanks. They have also retained the discipline, structure, and military effectiveness of the Guard. All that remains is war and a firm conviction: no one will send them to their deaths simply because “the regulations demand it.”<br/><br/>While still loyal to the Imperium, they clashed with the Tau on the battlefield. And they witnessed something that shook them to their core: precision coordination, drones that caused no casualties, tactics where every shot counted, and soldiers who weren’t sent into the meat grinder for the sake of dubious victories. After several campaigns, they realized: the Imperium had fallen behind forever. And so they decided to forge their own path for humanity—without fanaticism, without senseless sacrifices, without faith in a divine corpse on a throne. The Xenarites, a radical sect of the Mechanicus, helped adapt the Tau’s lessons: captured drones, fire-coordination devices, and tactics. The Separatists do not want to become the Tau; they want to outlive the Imperium—and to do so, they are willing to learn from anyone. To them, xenotechnology is not heresy, but inspiration and new possibilities that they had previously failed to see behind the dogmas.<br/><br/>The separatists new state is brutal, just like any military dictatorship born in the heat of an uprising. Here, people aren’t shot for asking questions, but desertion is punishable by death. There are no churches here, but there are memorials to the fallen. The separatists are not fighting for glory or for gods. They are fighting for the only thing they have left—the right to decide for themselves what they are willing to die for. And if that means using captured drones or copying the tactics of the xenos—well, they do so with the enthusiasm of people who have finally been allowed to think for themselves."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsLostFlavor" value="It’s all over. The Gladius is lost. The sky no longer remembers our signals. The last vox has fallen silent. The Xenarites didn’t have time to save the blueprints. The Commissars didn’t have time to read the names. We didn’t die for the Emperor or for the gods. We died for a freedom that no one can take from us—not even now. Let our bodies mingle with the ashes of this planet. Let our names be erased from the archives of the Imperium. We were. We fought. We asked for no mercy. But somewhere in the galaxy, there are other worlds of the New Humanity. And there, someone will raise our banner. He will read our lists. And he will understand—we did not surrender!"/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsProperties" value="<style name='Title'/>Coordination Tactics<br/><icon texture='GUI/Bullet'/><style name='Bold'/>Initial Challenge:<style name='Default'/> <style color='GUI/Yellow'/>Medium<style color='Default'/><br/><icon texture='GUI/Bullet'/><style name='Bold'/>Defectors:<style name='Default'/> Infantry that maintains the discipline of the Astra Militarum but lacks fanaticism. Effective in defense and when supported by artillery.<br/><icon texture='GUI/Bullet'/><style name='Bold'/>Trophy drones:<style name='Default'/> The Xenarite have adapted Tau technology—combat drones that are effective at dealing damage and improve the accuracy of infantry when targeting highlighted targets.<br/><icon texture='GUI/Bullet'/><style name='Bold'/>Mobile infantry:<style name='Default'/> The modified “Chimeras” transport soldiers to the front lines more quickly, while the “Devil's Dogs” have been equipped with troop compartments for infantry.<br/><icon texture='GUI/Bullet'/><style name='Bold'/>Markerlight:<style name='Default'/> Mark enemy units for a more damaging attack.<br/><icon texture='GUI/Bullet'/><style name='Bold'/>Imperial Heritage:<style name='Default'/> The separatists use the same technology, buildings, and decrees as the guard, but without the religious rituals."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsShortFlavor" value="The Separatist Regiments are a breakaway faction of the Astra Militarum that has rejected the Imperial cult while retaining its discipline and military might. Having adopted the lessons of the Tau—not their faith, but their tactics—they fight for the only thing they have left: the right to freedom and to die by their own choice."/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsWonEliminationFlavor" value="Gladus Prime is ours. The Imperium has been driven from the planet. The xenos are dead or have fled. Chaos has been scattered. Our engineers are tallying the spoils, the xenarites are dissecting enemy technology, and the soldiers are sleeping without fear of being shot for retreating for the first time in a long while. We have won. Not in the name of the Emperor, not in the name of the Dark Gods, not in the name of the Greater Good—but simply because we wanted to survive on our own terms.<br/><br/>The new state keeps this world in order. Free people don’t need chains to maintain order. The arbiters keep order, but their power is limited by the law, not by the whims of a commissioner. The Soylents farms still feed us tasteless paste, but now no one pretends not to know what it’s made of. And that, perhaps, is freedom. Dirty, hungry, but ours! For all who fell! For those who survived! For the New Humanity!"/>
	<entry name="SeparatistRegimentsWonQuestFlavor" value=""/>
